

<% content_for :title, "CBT & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .cbt-bipolar-page .cbt-copy,
  .cbt-bipolar-page .cbt-distortions > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .cbt-bipolar-page .cbt-distortions span {
    display: block;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .cbt-bipolar-page .cbt-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>

<main class="cbt-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="cbt-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="cbt-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="cbt-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / CBT
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>CBT LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="cbt-bipolar-intro">
        YOUR BRAIN SAID IT.<br>
        THAT DOESN'T MAKE IT FUCKING TRUE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 / THOUGHTS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-pink" aria-labelledby="cbt-thoughts-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">01 / THOUGHTS</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-thoughts-title">
        THOUGHT<br>
        ≠ FACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <blockquote>
        <p>“You are imperfect, you are wired for struggle, but you are worthy of love and belonging.”</p>
        <cite class="cbt-source">—Dr. Brené Brown</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Cognitive Behavioral Therapy—CBT—looks at how thoughts, emotions, physical
        sensations, and behaviors influence one another. An automatic thought can feel
        convincing before you have examined it. You can notice it, question it, and
        choose how to respond.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar symptoms cannot simply be controlled by thinking differently. CBT helps
        you examine the interpretations and habits that influence your experience.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / AUTOMATIC THOUGHTS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-yellow" aria-labelledby="cbt-automatic-thoughts-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">02 / AUTOMATIC THOUGHTS</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-automatic-thoughts-title">
        CATCH THE<br>
        FIRST STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        When a thought arrives, pause and ask, “Are you sure?” Acknowledge the emotion
        without immediately accepting the interpretation. Check the evidence, consider
        what you do not know, and look for a more accurate explanation.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        EVIDENCED BY WHAT?
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-black" aria-labelledby="cbt-distortions-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">03 / COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-distortions-title">
        YOUR BRAIN<br>
        HAS SOME<br>
        FAVORITES.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        Cognitive distortions: some of your brain’s favorite ways to twist a situation.
        Common patterns include:
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-distortions">

        <div>
          <span>ABSOLUTES</span>
          <p>Always, never, everyone.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ALL-OR-NOTHING</span>
          <p>Only success or failure.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>BLAMING</span>
          <p>Placing too much responsibility on others.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CATASTROPHIZING</span>
          <p>Expecting the worst possible outcome.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DISCOUNTING THE POSITIVE</span>
          <p>Dismissing accomplishments and progress.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>EMOTIONAL REASONING</span>
          <p>Feeling it makes it true.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>FORTUNE TELLING</span>
          <p>Treating predictions as facts.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>JUMPING TO CONCLUSIONS</span>
          <p>Deciding without enough evidence.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>LABELING</span>
          <p>Letting a mistake define a person.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>MAGNIFICATION / MINIMIZATION</span>
          <p>Negatives grow; positives shrink.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>MENTAL FILTER</span>
          <p>Focusing only on the negative.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>MIND READING</span>
          <p>Assuming you know others’ thoughts.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>OVERGENERALIZATION</span>
          <p>One experience becomes the rule.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>PERSONALIZATION</span>
          <p>Taking excessive responsibility.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SHOULD / MUST</span>
          <p>Rigid rules about how life should be.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>UNFAIR COMPARISONS</span>
          <p>Your weaknesses against others’ strengths.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        Catch the thought, name the distortion, and reframe it using the evidence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / BIPOLAR EPISODES -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-purple" aria-labelledby="cbt-episodes-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">04 / BIPOLAR EPISODES</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-episodes-title">
        MOOD CAN<br>
        CHANGE THE<br>
        STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        During mania, confidence and urgency can make an idea feel unquestionably right.
        While stable, prepare boundaries around spending, socializing, major decisions,
        and sleep.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mixed symptoms can combine agitation and racing thoughts with hopelessness.
        Notice automatic thoughts, delay decisions when highly activated, use grounding,
        and follow your established episode plan. Ask for support when symptoms become
        difficult to manage. An intense emotion does not require an immediate action.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / GUILT & SHAME -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-pink" aria-labelledby="cbt-guilt-shame-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">05 / GUILT & SHAME</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-guilt-shame-title">
        OWN IT.<br>
        REPAIR.<br>
        GROW.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        Guilt says, “I did something harmful.” Shame says, “I am a harmful person.”
        Guilt can motivate accountability, repair, and change. Shame turns a behavior
        into a verdict on your entire identity. Acknowledge the consequences of your
        actions while remembering that they do not describe everything you are.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 / IMPULSIVITY -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-yellow" aria-labelledby="cbt-impulsivity-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">06 / IMPULSIVITY</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-impulsivity-title">
        AH,<br>
        IMPULSIVITY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        Ah, impulsivity. When an urge promises relief, notice its story: “I deserve
        this,” “Just this once,” or “I’ll deal with the consequences later.”
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what you hope the action will accomplish, what might happen afterward, and
        whether the choice reflects your long-term values or your current emotional
        state. Set boundaries before you need them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 / BEHAVIOR -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-purple" aria-labelledby="cbt-behavior-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">07 / BEHAVIOR</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-behavior-title">
        DO<br>
        SOMETHING<br>
        DIFFERENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        Behavioral activation means taking manageable action even when motivation is
        absent. Break the task down and do one small thing. Small actions count toward
        progress. You do not have to tackle everything at once.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 / CORE BELIEFS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-black" aria-labelledby="cbt-core-beliefs-title">
    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">08 / CORE BELIEFS</p>
      <h2 id="cbt-core-beliefs-title">
        CHECK YOUR<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        THINKING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">
      <p>
        Beliefs like “I’m difficult to love” or “I’ll never have a normal life” can
        harden after episodes and stigma. Ask whether the evidence supports such
        sweeping conclusions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Acknowledge your limitations, take responsibility for your treatment, and leave
        room for growth. Your diagnosis and your past do not determine your entire worth
        or future.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="cbt-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="cbt-ending-small">
        YOUR BRAIN HAS OPINIONS.
      </p>

      <h2>
        CHECK THE<br>
        <span>RECEIPTS.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="cbt-ending-copy">

      <p>
        CBT offers ways to examine thoughts and behaviors and respond with greater
        awareness. It does not promise to prevent every episode. The goal is a
        meaningful life shaped by more than your diagnosis.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "cbt-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>


